  "title": {
    "en": "Bad photos and jizz"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Hawks, falcons, a cuckoo and a swift: naming a bird from its shape alone"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Most birds that you see are poor views: far away, against the light, in a hurry or half behind a branch. This lesson is about reading shape and movement. Experienced birders call it jizz: the 'general impression of size and shape' that tells them the species before they see a single field mark.\n\nThe test is the group that most often shows up as a dark shape in the sky: the hawks and the falcons. The Sparrowhawk, Goshawk, Peregrine, Hobby, Merlin and Kestrel all hunt birds or small animals on the wing, and each has a distinctive silhouette and wingbeat. We add the Cuckoo, which looks like a hawk, and the Alpine Swift, which looks like a big, pale Swift.\n\nThe photos in this lesson are deliberately not perfect: silhouettes, backlit birds, distant views. In the quiz, the answers are other birds of the same shape, so the pros' advice applies: do not guess from colour, look at the wings, the tail and the way it flies."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "silhouette"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The outline of a bird against the sky, with no colour or detail. Wing and tail shape alone often tells the species."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wingbeat"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The rhythm and depth of the wing strokes. A Hobby's is quick and scythe-like, a Sparrowhawk's is flap, flap, glide."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "jizz"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The overall impression of a bird: its size, shape, movement and behaviour together."
      }
    }
  ],
